merge("entities/benny-gantz.md", u"""
## Withdrawal from the war cabinet (June 2024)

On the evening of Sunday, June 9, 2024, Gantz announced the withdrawal of his centrist National Unity party from Israel's emergency wartime government, accusing Prime Minister [[benjamin-netanyahu|Benjamin Netanyahu]] of preventing Israel from achieving "true victory" in the war against [[hamas|Hamas]] and urging new elections.[{N}] The move came weeks after Gantz had conditioned his continued support on the prime minister's acceptance of an agreed-upon vision for the Gaza conflict by June 8.[{N}] Gantz was one of three voting members of Netanyahu's war cabinet.[{N}]

Explaining the decision, Gantz said that "after October 7, just like hundreds of thousands of patriotic Israelis, my colleagues and I mobilized as well" and joined the coalition "even though we knew it was a bad government."[{N}] He added that the party had done so "because we knew it was a bad government," because "the people of Israel, the fighters, the commanders, the families of the murdered, the casualties and the hostages needed unity and support like they needed air to breathe."[{N}] National Unity had joined the emergency government days after October 7, 2023, when Hamas-led terrorists stormed southern Israel, killing some 1,200 people, mostly civilians, and taking 251 hostages into the Gaza Strip.[{N}]

## Badlands reading of the resignation

[[ghostofbasedpatrickhenry|GhostofBasedPatrickHenry]] treated the resignation as "too perfect," noting the universal consensus that Gantz was the moderate in the room — he had run against Netanyahu on a moderate platform in the most recent election — and that his departure would leave Netanyahu's security cabinet dominated by Zionist-maximalists openly calling for the conquest of Palestine.[{N}] The commentary tied this to a claimed December 2021 [[donald-trump|Donald Trump]] media campaign on Israel's Channel 12 in which Trump promoted Gantz and [[mahmoud-abbas|Mahmoud Abbas]] as the region's two genuine peacemakers, saying "Gantz loves Israel" and expressing puzzlement that Netanyahu remained in government, since he had expected Gantz to take over after a fixed period.[{N}] Trump was quoted as saying that if Gantz had won the election, peace "would be a lot easier," and that "I don't think Bibi ever wanted to make peace; never did."[{N}]
""")

merge("entities/ken-paxton.md", u"""
## Investigation into automaker data collection (June 2024)

In June 2024 Paxton opened an investigation into several car manufacturers following widespread reporting that they had secretly been collecting mass amounts of data about drivers directly from their vehicles and then selling that data to third parties, including insurance providers.[{N}] The inquiry was brought under the [[texas-deceptive-trade-practices-act|Texas Deceptive Trade Practices – Consumer Protection Act]], which authorizes the Office of the Attorney General to investigate false, misleading, or deceptive acts or practices.[{N}] Car manufacturers and the third parties to whom they sold data were instructed to produce documents relevant to their conduct, as well as documents showing what disclosures they had made to customers about the extent of their data collection and subsequent sale.[{N}]

Paxton said that "the technology in modern vehicles enables manufacturers to collect millions of data points about the people driving them," that consumers had "grown extremely concerned that their driving data is being reported to their insurance company without their knowledge or authorization," and that reports of "invasive and unmitigated collection and sale of data without consumer consent" merited "a thorough investigation and appropriate enforcement."[{N}]

[[ashe-in-america|Ashe in America]] praised the action, arguing that [[data-freedom|data freedom]] is the civil rights issue of the era and that too few officials with statutory authority are willing to fight for it, ostensibly because they benefit from data harvesting; she credited Paxton with being one of the exceptions.[{N}]
""")

merge("entities/emmerson-mnangagwa.md", u"""
## Remarks at SPIEF 2024

Speaking to [[tass|TASS]] on the sidelines of the [[2024-st-petersburg-international-economic-forum|St. Petersburg International Economic Forum]] in June 2024, Mnangagwa said [[zimbabwe|Zimbabwe]] was grateful to Russia for supplies of grain and fertilizer and that the country would have "an incredible harvest" that year "because we have enough fertilizer."[{N}] He added that Zimbabwe would need more supplies, that "Russia gives us lower prices and convenient payment methods than anywhere else," and that his government was "happy with the existing agreements and opportunities."[{N}] At the same forum he declared that "Africa will soon roar like a lion."[{N}]

[[ghostofbasedpatrickhenry|GhostofBasedPatrickHenry]] cited the remarks as evidence of the power of bilateral agreements over what he called elitist trade organizations that skim off the top, arguing that any competitor wishing to take that market must now offer Zimbabwe better value.[{N}]
""")

merge("entities/zimbabwe.md", u"""
## Russian fertilizer supplies and SPIEF 2024

In June 2024 President [[emmerson-mnangagwa|Emmerson Mnangagwa]] told [[tass|TASS]] at the [[2024-st-petersburg-international-economic-forum|St. Petersburg International Economic Forum]] that Zimbabwe had received both grain and fertilizer from Russia and expected an incredible harvest as a result, while noting that Russia offered lower prices and more convenient payment methods than any alternative supplier.[{N}] A humanitarian shipment of 23,000 tons of Uralchem fertilizers had been delivered and transferred to Zimbabwe in March of that year.[{N}]

## Colonial history in Badlands commentary

[[ghostofbasedpatrickhenry|GhostofBasedPatrickHenry]] recounted that Zimbabwe was formerly called Rhodesia, named in 1895 by the British South Africa Company after [[cecil-rhodes|Cecil Rhodes]], whom he described as an agent of the Rothschild banking family dispatched to Africa in the 1880s to build the "Cape to Cairo" railway connecting the north African coast to South Africa.[{N}] He noted that Rhodesia shared a long border with German East Africa and invited readers to imagine how local populations were treated by British and Prussian prospectors.[{N}] He further characterized the [[rhodes-scholarship|Rhodes Scholarship]] as a program used to identify and cultivate talent for what he called the globalist mafia, citing Bill Clinton and Pete Buttigieg as alumni.[{N}] The commentary concluded that Russia continues to gain ground in Africa through such arrangements.[{N}]
""")
